Z||000003||theogre||04-04-99||02:54 AM||||just call me ogre,,,,,,,,<p>no. I don't think it's going to make that much diferance, but if he want's to try, I figure he might as well not break PVC all over the highway. Enough crud out there already.<p>The biggest problems with the Fiero motors isn't the intakes. It's those lame as hell exhaust manifolds. I don't think the intakes flow enough volume, at enough speed, to really have turbulence problems. Those crudy exhaust manifolds on the other hand..............
Z||000004||Pretender||04-04-99||04:02 AM||||I hope i don't sound cocky but I too have read that artical on that website, if it were tested on a dyno then any results that were taken had to be off..sitting still the air doesn't flow at the same rate as it would if the car was only going 5mph..thanks for the advise.  I will test this at the track on sat. before i install the pully and the chip. to prove that it should work..the toyota MR2 has the same problem, i read about it in a "sport compact car"., sitting still on the dyno produced in-accurate numbers, but when a large fan was located infront if the air inlet the hp rose...i really do think this will work...thanks...
Z||000005||Motorhead||04-04-99||04:38 AM||||Seems to me the most restrictive component in the intake is the air filter.What do you plan on using to clean the air? <p>My Z Car friends are reporting significant performance increases with ram air systems using K&N filters.<p>I hadn't thought about a ram air system for my Fiero,but after readng your post I think I'll investigate this further.<p>Let us know how you make out.
Z||000006||Shaun41178||04-04-99||05:58 PM||||I have had my car on a chassis dyno and I threw togethor a cold air intake real quick with a K&N cone filter.  i picked up 2 peak hp but lost 4 lb-ft of torque.  This was with a really bad misfire so I am guessing I can get +2 more hp at higher rpm and hopefully get some torque back.  Results might be a little better on the highway where the air is moving good.  Try to get the filter down in front of the wheel well and completely remove the entire stock air intake assembly.  With my setup I just used the stock "S" tube and hooked the filter to the end of it.  There is a little room for improvement but don't expect more than 5-6 hp increase.  hopefully no torque is lost like mine cause that is more important on the street then hp.  I still have some more work to do on my intake setup and pics will be on my website soon.
Z||000007||Pretender||04-04-99||08:52 PM||||As of now(stock) i'm using the drop in K&N from the fiero store...when this is done i will be using a cone K&N filter placed right behind thefactory air scoop...i would like to get the side scoops from "IRM" but there a little salty at $260..hopefully i can find some at the next fiero swap meet.
Z||000008||DXR_DAD||04-05-99||09:51 PM||||Ram Air set-up on my 86 SE, used the stock "S" tube ( cut about 3" at angle off end to stock box, remove complete box) Used K&N cone,turned S tube so cone is right at intake hole. Looking for ram air scoops but will try little hood scoop from 260Z??<br>No dyno test but I can feel the differance.<p>And it sounds good.
Z||000009||theogre||04-05-99||11:29 PM||||Some of you guys don't get it. You CANNOT check the installation of a modification to the Fiero air inlet on a dyno. The car must be in moving air. Which meens either the car is moving, or the dyno is in a wind tunnel.<p>1. Ram air intakes ONLY work right if air is forced into them. A standard chassis dyno CANNOT do this therefore any numbers you get WILL be wrong compaired to proper operation of the intake.<p>2. Air fowing across the end of a tube, like the fiero stock intake, usually causes a hell of alot of turbulence, and very often a vacume. You can test this yourself by blowing across the end of a straw with the other end in your drink. DON'T do this pointing at your girlfriend/wife/etc unless you want to be killed. Again dyno numbers will be off because there is no air flow over the car. (Likewise a stiff cross wind hitting the left side of the can do some interesting things when tooling down the highway.)<p>Dyno testing of Fiero IS reasonably valid for nearly all other changes you can make to the car, BUT you must always remember that final numbers are affected by the intake, and how fast air is moving into, or over, it.<br>
Z||000010||Shaun41178||04-06-99||01:07 AM||||Ogre no flaming here but this topic is not about Ram air intake.  this is about Cold air intake.  I understand that Ram air can not be tested unless out on hte street.  But for some edification I tested my cold air intake and got a +3 hp on my G-tech and got a +2 hp on the chassis dyno.  This was with a K&N cone filter mounted on the "s" tube.  A couple more hp might be available at speed but it ain't going to be much.<p>Ram air is going to be diffucult to devise because the air does not get rammed into the filter because the filter is pulling air from the side of the car instead of the front like on a trans am.   The only real way to get ram air is stick the filter out in the stream of airflow.  There is no real place to stick the filter out into the air stream with the way the intake system is from the factory.  Other then that the filter is only sucking in hte air and none is being rammed in.<br>
Z||000011||Pretender||04-06-99||01:51 AM||||a cone filter stuck on the end of the "s" tube is not classified as a cold air intake., there is no cold out side air getting to it...the filter is still in the engine bay which is a long way from being cool. I am placing the K&N cone filter directly behind the factory inlet.  which is replaced by irm side scoops.  aka: cold air intake, and i know that it will out flow the factory unit or the filter on the "s" tube. take my advise and try it before you knock it....whats to lose?..
Z||000012||Shaun41178||04-07-99||12:07 AM||||Pretender, my system was just a preliminary test to see if any more power could be gained from the intake system.  Everyone says that more hp can not be obtained by modifying the intake.  i just wanted to see if it was possible.  And since I gained 2 hp then this proves that it is possible.  However I do plan on mounting the filter down in front of the tire here soon.  I currently have put my factory system back on for now as I am not yet done collecting all the parts to assemble the filter outside the engien compartment.  Good luck with your cold air system.
Z||000013||Pretender||04-07-99||01:33 AM||||it appears that we have the same objectives..i know that the power is there, alas everyone is skepical, before i decided to get the fiero running i was into the imports(still am)...it's almost the same concept as they use....keep in touch on how yours turns out...well compare notes.
Z||000014||theogre||04-07-99||12:52 PM||||It would seem to me, that if you are going to use a scoop then you would get better results with a filter box sealed to the back of it. If I'm reading this right, you guys are just laying the filter into the area behind the inlet. In that case most of the air being collected by the scoop is just blowing by it, and into the fender area. Doesn't seem like you'd get much benifit from the scoop like that.<p>What I'd like to know myself is do the scoops  actually have enough flow rammed into them to really make a diferance. The Fiero intake location seems like it would be in some farily turbulant air. One of these days I'll have to tape on some tatlers and see how laminar the air flow is down the side of the car. If it's fairly smooth a properly designed scoop could ram a hell of allot of air. The question I guess would then be is any scoop "off the shelf" designed right to take maximum advantage of the flow over the side.<p>On the other hand I'd be interested in seeing if the airflow over the side is smooth enough that it could actually be causing reduced intake thru the duct. Air flow over the end of a tube has a nasty habbit of causing a preasure drop in the tube. Might not be enough to kill the engine, but it could hurt overall performance.
